{"reference":"Isaiah 9","book":"Isaiah","chapter":9,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Reading the royal poem","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Isaiah 9:1-7 speaks in royal and Davidic terms of a hoped-for rule marked by justice and peace. Its first historical referent, dating, and relationship to later messianic readings are disputed. A careful reading distinguishes the poem’s own literary and historical setting from later claims of prediction, typology, quotation, or broader canonical interpretation.","verse_refs":["Isaiah 9:1-7"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_isaiah_009_beec286e3e5f9c73f0545f76"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"Isaiah’s textual transmission","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"The Great Isaiah Scroll is an almost complete witness to all sixty-six chapters of Isaiah. It broadly aligns with the Masoretic tradition while also containing thousands of spelling and substantive differences. Other Isaiah manuscripts and interpretive writings from Qumran likewise show that Isaiah was actively copied and interpreted.","provenance_refs":["reader_path_isaiah_009_0d9744bee20e23b0201b2328"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}